    description: "Compare medications, counseling, overdose prevention, and different care settings for opioid use disorder.",
    overview: "Opioid use disorder is a treatable medical condition involving a problematic pattern of opioid use that causes impairment or distress. Treatment may be delivered through primary care, an opioid treatment program, an outpatient clinic, telehealth, residential care, or a hospital, depending on clinical needs and local rules.",
    evidence: "Methadone, buprenorphine, and naltrexone are FDA-approved medications for opioid use disorder, and SAMHSA recommends offering evidence-based medication as part of care. Methadone and buprenorphine reduce opioid use and support retention, while naltrexone requires completion of opioid withdrawal before initiation.",
