    uniqueSections: [
      { heading: "Medication is the foundation of opioid treatment", body: "Methadone and buprenorphine can reduce illicit opioid use and support retention, while naltrexone works differently and requires complete withdrawal before starting. The best choice depends on access, previous response, health, preference, and the ability to begin safely." },
      { heading: "Counseling can help without becoming a gate", body: "Therapy, peer support, and practical services may address trauma, relationships, housing, or daily routines. Patients should not lose life-saving medication simply because they cannot attend a particular group or because one counseling approach does not fit." },
      { heading: "Starting care should be fast and individualized", body: "The assessment reviews last use, withdrawal, fentanyl exposure, other sedatives, pregnancy, pain, overdose, and current medicines. Buprenorphine timing and methadone dosing require clinical decisions, and follow-up should be frequent enough to adjust when cravings or withdrawal continue." },
      { heading: "Overdose prevention remains part of treatment", body: "Naloxone should be available to the patient and close contacts, with clear instructions to call 911 and repeat doses when needed. Treatment gaps, incarceration, hospitalization, detoxification, or stopping medication can reduce tolerance and make a return to use especially dangerous." },
      { heading: "Access rules differ across the three medications", body: "Methadone for opioid use disorder is generally provided through a certified opioid treatment program, while buprenorphine can be prescribed in office-based settings. Ask about take-home doses, pharmacy access, travel, required visits, drug testing, and the process for transferring care." },
      { heading: "Create a continuity plan before problems occur", body: "Write down the prescriber, pharmacy or dosing site, after-hours contact, insurance steps, and backup provider. Discuss what happens after a missed dose, emergency admission, move, pregnancy, or lost medication so an administrative problem does not become an untreated withdrawal or overdose risk." },
      { heading: "Duration should follow benefit and patient goals", body: "There is no universal deadline for stopping medication for opioid use disorder, and some people benefit from long-term or indefinite treatment. A taper should be voluntary, gradual, and paired with close monitoring and a way to restart promptly if risk increases. Pressure from family, housing, court, or a program should be discussed openly with the prescriber. Stability on medication is a successful treatment outcome, not a reason to force a change." },
    ],
